 ## Background
-The concept of fixed-step ray marching for volume rendering originated in early computer graphics literature as a straightforward method for integrating volume density along rays [S1, unofficial]. The heuristic of using the projected pixel size to determine step size was introduced to mitigate aliasing by ensuring that the sampling rate matches the spatial frequency of the image [S1, unofficial]. This method is widely used in real-time rendering, including WebGL and WebGPU samples, due to its simplicity and hardware friendliness [S3, unofficial]. It is a foundational technique in the family of distance-field ray marching methods [S1, unofficial].
